    "https://www.careers.luc.edu/postings/35925": {
      "title": "Associate Dean for Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Assessment",
      "description": "Loyola University Chicago Career Site | Associate Dean for Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Assessment Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities Designed to Help Us Collaborate in Creation! Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Onboarding Professional Development FAQs Contact Us Associate Dean for Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Assessment Thank you for your interest in Loyola University Chicago. To view open positions, please enter your search criteria below. You may view all open positions by not specifying any search criteria and selecting the \"Search\" button. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin in October 2026 and continue until the positions are filled. Applicants should submit the following through careers.luc.edu: Cover letter (up to two pages) describing your interest in the position; qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor; and leadership experience in nursing education, competency-based teaching and assessment, and faculty development. Teaching and Educational Leadership Statement (up to three pages) describing your approach to strengthening faculty expertise in evidence-based teaching and competency-based assessment aligned with the AACN Essentials. Include examples of initiatives you have led, their outcomes, and how you collaborated with faculty and academic leaders. Describe your vision for this faculty-focused, consultative role and how your educational scholarship and national engagement would contribute to Loyola Nursing’s visibility and leadership in nursing education. Curriculum vitae . Mission and Inclusive Excellence Statement (up to two pages) describing how your leadership would advance Loyola University Chicago’s Jesuit, Catholic mission and Loyola Nursing’s commitment to belonging and inclusive excellence. Please address: Understanding: How do you understand the relationship among Loyola’s Jesuit, Catholic mission, transformative education, and inclusive excellence in nursing? Past contributions: How have you fostered belonging and advanced inclusive excellence through educational leadership, faculty development, teaching, mentoring, scholarship, or service? Future plans: How would you advance Loyola’s mission, belonging, and inclusive excellence through your leadership, faculty support, teaching, and scholarship at Loyola Nursing? Names and contact information for three professional references who can speak to your educational leadership, scholarship, and ability to work collaboratively with faculty and academic leaders. References will be contacted only for finalists. Questions about the positions may be directed to Todd Ruppar, chair of the search committee, at truppar@luc.edu Position Details Position Details Job Title Associate Dean for Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Assessment Position Title Associate Dean for Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Assessment Position Number Job Category University Faculty Job Type Full-Time FLSA Status Exempt Campus Maywood-Health Sciences & Rogers Park-Lake Shore Campuses Location Code SCHOOL OF NURSING (02400A) Department Name SCHOOL OF NURSING Is this split and/or fully grant funded? No Duties and Responsibilities Salary Range: $100,000 – $147,000 Benefits Information: https://www.luc.edu/hr/benefits/ The Loyola University Chicago Marcella Niehoff School of Nursing (Loyola Nursing) invites applications for an Associate Dean of Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Assessment. The Associate Dean for Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Education advances excellence and innovation in nursing education by strengthening faculty expertise in competency-based teaching and assessment, evidence-based pedagogy, and educational scholarship. The Associate Dean provides strategic leadership, consultation, and professional learning that help faculty translate curricular competencies into effective teaching, assessment, feedback, and evaluation of student learning across Loyola Nursing. Through collaborative leadership, the Associate Dean strengthens faculty capacity to apply competency-based, evidence-informed approaches consistent with the AACN Essentials, professional and accreditation standards, institutional priorities, and emerging practices in nursing education. The Associate Dean supports faculty at all career stages and advances Loyola Nursing’s national visibility in competency-based education, evidence-based teaching, learner assessment, and educational scholarship. The Associate Dean reports to the Dean, serves on the Dean’s Senior Leadership Team, and contributes to school-wide strategy and decision-making. This is a 12-month administrative appointment, with 50% effort in administration and 50% in teaching, scholarship, and service. Faculty responsibilities follow a nine-month academic calendar. The role is faculty-focused and consultative. Academic program administration, accreditation, simulation, educational technologies, clinical education, and other established areas remain the responsibility of the leaders assigned to them. The Associate Dean collaborates with faculty and academic leaders to strengthen teaching and assessment across programs. Responsibilities Mission and Strategic Vision: Advances a school-wide vision for faculty excellence in competency-based teaching and assessment that reflects Loyola Nursing’s mission, institutional priorities, and emerging national standards and practices. Competency-Based Education: Helps faculty translate curricular competencies, as established by academic programs, into effective teaching strategies, learning experiences, assessment methods, feedback, and evaluation of student learning Teaching Excellence and Faculty Capacity: Strengthens faculty capacity at all career stages to use evidence-based, inclusive, and learner-centered teaching practices. Leads professional learning in competency-based education, learner assessment, student engagement, active and case-based learning, reflective teaching, and feedback. Assessment Expertise: Builds faculty expertise in assessment design, item writing and analysis, rubric development, competency evaluation, calibration, feedback, and interpretation of student-learning data. Promotes shared understanding and consistent use of effective assessment practices in collaboration with the leaders responsible for program-level assessment and evaluation. Consultation and Coaching: Provides individual and group consultation on competency-based teaching, learner assessment, feedback, and evaluation of student learning. Partners with appropriate leaders when faculty needs intersect with program oversight, workload, evaluation, performance, or personnel responsibilities. Academic Leadership Collaboration: Partners with the Executive Associate Dean for Academic Affairs and academic program leaders to align faculty development with program needs and with established curricular, accreditation, and institutional priorities. Collaborates with the Associate Dean for Faculty Affairs so that teaching-focused professional development complements faculty onboarding, mentoring, evaluation, promotion, and career development. Collaboration with Innovative Educational Strategies and Simulation: Partners with the Assistant Dean for Innovative Educational Strategies and Simulation to connect faculty development in competency-based teaching and assessment with simulation, experiential learning, and educational technology. The Assistant Dean retains leadership for the development, implementation, and evaluation of innovative educational strategies, simulation, and educational technologies. The Associate Dean provides consultation regarding competency alignment, teaching practices, and learner assessment. Scholarship and Visibility: Supports faculty scholarship, dissemination, publication, and grant development related to teaching, assessment, and competency-based education, in coordination with the Associate Dean for Research and Scholarly Innovation. Fosters collaborative educational scholarship and builds Loyola Nursing’s national visibility in these areas. Evaluation and Continuous Advancement: Evaluates the reach, effectiveness, and faculty application of professional development within the position’s portfolio. Monitors emerging evidence and national developments in competency-based education, teaching, and learner assessment to guide priorities and strengthen faculty support. Qualifications Qualified for appointment at the rank of associate professor or professor (clinical or tenure-track) Demonstrated excellence and leadership in nursing education, including competency-based teaching and assessment aligned with the AACN Essentials Sustained record of educational scholarship and dissemination Demonstrated ability to lead faculty development and collaborate across programs Physical Demands None Working Conditions None Minimum Education and/or Work Experience • Doctoral degree in nursing or a related field • Eligibility for licensure as a registered nurse in the state of Illinois Open Date 06/30/2027 Close Date Salary Range $100,000-$147,000 Additional Salary Information This salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. As Associate Dean for Teaching Excellence and Competency-Based Assessment, an administrative stipend will be added for a twelve-month service basis. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on a range of factors including, but not limited to, the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including years since terminal degree; training; field or discipline; budget availability; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Special Instructions to Applicants Review of applications will begin in October 2026 and continue until the positions are filled. Applicants should submit the following through careers.luc.edu: Cover letter (up to two pages) describing your interest in the position; qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor; and leadership experience in nursing education, competency-based teaching and assessment, and faculty development. Teaching and Educational Leadership Statement (up to three pages) describing your approach to strengthening faculty expertise in evidence-based teaching and competency-based assessment aligned with the AACN Essentials. Include examples of initiatives you have led, their outcomes, and how you collaborated with faculty and academic leaders. Describe your vision for this faculty-focused, consultative role and how your educational scholarship and national engagement would contribute to Loyola Nursing’s visibility and leadership in nursing education. Curriculum vitae . Mission and Inclusive Excellence Statement (up to two pages) describing how your leadership would advance Loyola University Chicago’s Jesuit, Catholic mission and Loyola Nursing’s commitment to belonging and inclusive excellence. Please address: Understanding: How do you understand the relationship among Loyola’s Jesuit, Catholic mission, transformative education, and inclusive excellence in nursing? Past contributions: How have you fostered belonging and advanced inclusive excellence through educational leadership, faculty development, teaching, mentoring, scholarship, or service? Future plans: How would you advance Loyola’s mission, belonging, and inclusive excellence through your leadership, faculty support, teaching, and scholarship at Loyola Nursing? Names and contact information for three professional references who can speak to your educational leadership, scholarship, and ability to work collaboratively with faculty and academic leaders. References will be contacted only for finalists. Questions about the positions may be directed to Todd Ruppar, chair of the search committee, at truppar@luc.edu About Loyola University Chicago Founded in 1870, Loyola University Chicago is one of the nation’s largest Jesuit, Catholic universities, recognized for its academic excellence, commitment to community engagement, and leadership in sustainability. A Carnegie R1 research institution, Loyola leverages its status as one of an elite group of universities with the highest level of research activity to advance knowledge that serves communities and creates global impact. With 15 schools, colleges, and institutes —including Business, Law, Medicine, Nursing, and Health Sciences—Loyola operates three primary campuses in the greater Chicago area and one in Rome, Italy, that provide students a transformative, globally connected learning experience. Consistently ranked among the nation’s top universities by U.S. News & World Report, Loyola is a STARS Gold-rated institution that is ranked as one of the country’s most sustainable campuses by The Princeton Review and has earned distinctions from AmeriCorps and the Carnegie Foundation for its longstanding record of service and community engagement. Guided by its Jesuit mission and commitment to caring for the whole person, Loyola educates ethical leaders who think critically, act with purpose, and strive to create a more just and sustainable world. Loyola University Chicago strives to be an employer of choice by offering its staff and faculty a wide array of affordable, comprehensive, and competitive benefits. To view our benefits in detail, click here . Loyola adheres to all applicable federal, state, and/or local civil rights laws and regulations prohibiting discrimination in private institutions of higher education. Please see the University’s Nondiscrimination Policy . About the Marcella Niehoff School of Nursing Loyola University Chicago’s Marcella Niehoff School of Nursing advances the science of nursing and provides a transformative education in the Jesuit Catholic tradition that prepares compassionate, innovative, diverse nurse leaders who provide care for the whole person and partner locally and globally to promote social justice and health for all. Our vision is to be a preeminent leader in transforming the health of persons, families, communities, and populations while promoting social justice, health equity, and quality care for all. A leader in nursing education for more than 90 years, Loyola Nursing is a community of approximately 80 full-time faculty, 26 staff, and more than 1,300 students. Our programs include the BSN and Master’s Entry to Nursing (MN) programs for prelicensure students, a DNP with nurse practitioner tracks, and a PhD in Nursing. Loyola Nursing spans the University’s Lake Shore Campus and Health Sciences Campus. The Health Sciences Campus is also home to the Stritch School of Medicine, the Parkinson School of Health Sciences and Public Health, biomedical graduate programs, and Loyola’s health sciences research enterprise, including the 225,000-square-foot Center for Translational Research and Education. Loyola Nursing has earned national recognition for nursing education, research, and inclusive excellence. Recent distinctions include: A #19 national ranking for our BSN program in the 2027 U.S. News & World Report rankings, placing it among the top 3% of BSN programs nationwide. A #43 ranking among schools of nursing for National Institutes of Health funding in 2025. The 2026 American Association of Colleges of Nursing ( AACN ) Exemplary Academic-Practice Partnership Award, recognizing Loyola Nursing’s partnership with Northwestern Medicine. The 2024 AACN Inclusive Excellence, Belonging, and Sustainability in Nursing Education Award. Higher Education Excellence and Distinction ( HEED ) Awards for Health Professions Schools in 2023, 2024, 2025, and 2026. A new building scheduled to open on the Lake Shore Campus in 2028 will support the continued expansion of our BSN program. For more information, visit luc.edu/nursing. As a Jesuit Catholic institution of higher education, we seek candidates who will contribute to our strategic plan to deliver a Transformative Education in the Jesuit tradition. To learn more about Loyola University Chicago’s mission, candidates should consult our website at www.luc.edu/mission/. For information about the university’s focus on transformative education, they should consult our website at www.luc.edu/transformativeed. Quick Link for Posting https://www.careers.luc.edu/postings/35904 Job Number 8500300 Organizational Location HEALTH SCIENCES CAMPUS Posting Details Posting Detail Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date 07/01/2027 Position End Date Open Until Filled Yes Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Inclusive Excellence Statement Optional Documents Other Document Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Do you currently hold, or are you eligible for, RN licensure in the State of Illinois? Yes No Where did you hear about this position?\r\n•\tColleague\r\n•\tAmerican Association of Colleges of Nursing\r\n•\tNational Organization of Nurse Practitioner Faculties\r\n•\tMidwest Nursing Research Society\r\n•\tSouthern Nursing Research Society\r\n•\tNational Black Nurses Association\r\n•\tNational Association of Hispanic Nurses\r\n•\tPhilipine Nurses Association of America\r\n•\tAmerican Academy of Nursing\r\n•\tNational Association of Diversity Officers in \r\n        Higher Education\r\n•\tChronicle of Higher Education\r\n•\tOther, please specify: (Open Ended Question) Loyola University Chicago | Information: (773) 508 - 6000 1032 W Sheridan Rd | Chicago, IL | 60660 2018 (c) Loyola University Chicago | University Policies Support LUC To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://www.careers.luc.edu/postings/35924": {
      "title": "Director of Family Nurse Practitioner with Emergency Specialty Track of the Doctor of Nursing Practice Program (Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor)",
      "description": "Loyola University Chicago Career Site | Director of Family Nurse Practitioner with Emergency Specialty Track of the Doctor of Nursing Practice Program (Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor) Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities Designed to Help Us Collaborate in Creation! Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Onboarding Professional Development FAQs Contact Us Director of Family Nurse Practitioner with Emergency Specialty Track of the Doctor of Nursing Practice Program (Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor) Thank you for your interest in Loyola University Chicago. To view open positions, please enter your search criteria below. You may view all open positions by not specifying any search criteria and selecting the \"Search\" button. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin in October 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit: 1. Cover letter (up to two pages) describing your interest in Loyola Nursing, qualifications for the position and rank you are seeking, relevant clinical expertise, and teaching and mentorship experience. 2. A curriculum vitae. 3. Mission and Inclusive Excellence Statement: Describe how your work as a faculty member would advance Loyola University Chicago’s Jesuit Catholic mission and Loyola Nursing’s commitment to belonging and inclusive excellence. Your statement should address: • Understanding: How do you understand the relationship between Loyola’s Jesuit Catholic mission, transformative education, and inclusive excellence in nursing? • Past contributions: Describe how you have fostered belonging and advanced inclusive excellence through teaching, mentoring, scholarship, clinical practice, outreach, or service. • Future plans: Explain how you would advance Loyola’s mission, belonging, and inclusive excellence through your teaching, scholarship, and service at Loyola Nursing. 4. The names and contact information of three professional references. References will be contacted only for finalists. Apply at careers.luc.edu . Questions about the positions may be directed to Moonju Lee, chair of the search committee, at mlee11@luc.edu . Position Details Position Details Job Title Director of Family Nurse Practitioner with Emergency Specialty Track of the Doctor of Nursing Practice Program (Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor Position Title Director of Family Nurse Practitioner with Emergency Specialty Track of the Doctor of Nursing Practice Program (Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor) Position Number Job Category University Faculty Job Type Full-Time FLSA Status Exempt Campus Maywood-Health Sciences & Rogers Park-Lake Shore Campuses Location Code SCHOOL OF NURSING (02400A) Department Name SCHOOL OF NURSING Is this split and/or fully grant funded? No Duties and Responsibilities Salary Range: $80,000 – $124,000 Benefits Information: https://www.luc.edu/hr/benefits/ The Loyola University Chicago Marcella Niehoff School of Nursing (Loyola Nursing) invites applications for a clinical-track faculty position serving as Director of the Family Nurse Practitioner ( FNP ) with Emergency Specialty Track within the Doctor of Nursing Practice ( DNP ) Program. Guided by the missions, values, and strategic priorities of Loyola University Chicago and Loyola Nursing, the Director collaborates with the Assistant Dean of Graduate Clinical Programs, faculty, and academic leaders to advance student learning, professional role preparation, curriculum quality, and inclusive excellence within the FNP with Emergency Specialty Track. This is a non-tenure-track position at the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor. Rank will be determined by the candidate’s qualifications and record of teaching, scholarship, and service. The Director reports to the Assistant Dean of Graduate Clinical Programs. This is a 12-month administrative appointment, with 30% effort in administration and 70% in teaching, scholarship, and service. Faculty responsibilities follow a nine-month academic calendar. The Track Director will lead the FNP with Emergency Specialty Track in collaboration with the Assistant Dean of Graduate Clinical Programs and track faculty to strengthen curriculum quality, support student learning and progression, prepare graduates for practice, and evaluate track outcomes to guide continuous improvement Faculty Responsibilities include: • Teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students according to their expertise and program needs, with primary teaching responsibilities in the DNP program. • Use evidence-informed, inclusive, and learner-centered teaching and assessment practices. • Collaborate with faculty and academic leaders to plan, improve, and evaluate courses and curricula. • Develop and disseminate scholarship that advances nursing practice and education. • Collaborate with interdisciplinary partners across Loyola and beyond. • Contribute service to the School, University, community, and nursing profession. • Advance School and University priorities related to belonging and inclusive excellence. Qualifications Qualifications • Current clinical practice as an FNP with Emergency Specialty, with an expectation to continue practice. • A record of scholarship or demonstrated potential to develop and sustain scholarship appropriate to the candidate’s career stage and proposed rank. • Teaching experience with FNP -Emergency students preferred Physical Demands Working Conditions Minimum Education and/or Work Experience • A doctoral degree in nursing, or a doctoral degree in a related field and a master’s degree in nursing. • National certification as an FNP with Emergency Specialty • Current Illinois RN and APRN licensure, or eligibility for both Open Date 10/06/2026 Close Date Salary Range $80,000-$124,000 Additional Salary Information This salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. As FNP -ER Track Director, an administrative stipend will be added for a twelve-month service basis. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on a range of factors including, but not limited to, the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including years since terminal degree; training; field or discipline; budgetavailability; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Special Instructions to Applicants Review of applications will begin in October 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit: 1. Cover letter (up to two pages) describing your interest in Loyola Nursing, qualifications for the position and rank you are seeking, relevant clinical expertise, and teaching and mentorship experience. 2. A curriculum vitae. 3. Mission and Inclusive Excellence Statement: Describe how your work as a faculty member would advance Loyola University Chicago’s Jesuit Catholic mission and Loyola Nursing’s commitment to belonging and inclusive excellence. Your statement should address: • Understanding: How do you understand the relationship between Loyola’s Jesuit Catholic mission, transformative education, and inclusive excellence in nursing? • Past contributions: Describe how you have fostered belonging and advanced inclusive excellence through teaching, mentoring, scholarship, clinical practice, outreach, or service. • Future plans: Explain how you would advance Loyola’s mission, belonging, and inclusive excellence through your teaching, scholarship, and service at Loyola Nursing. 4. The names and contact information of three professional references. References will be contacted only for finalists. Apply at careers.luc.edu . Questions about the positions may be directed to Moonju Lee, chair of the search committee, at mlee11@luc.edu . About Loyola University Chicago Founded in 1870, Loyola University Chicago is one of the nation’s largest Jesuit, Catholic universities, recognized for its academic excellence, commitment to community engagement, and leadership in sustainability. A Carnegie R1 research institution, Loyola leverages its status as one of an elite group of universities with the highest level of research activity to advance knowledge that serves communities and creates global impact. With 15 schools, colleges, and institutes —including Business, Law, Medicine, Nursing, and Health Sciences—Loyola operates three primary campuses in the greater Chicago area and one in Rome, Italy, that provide students a transformative, globally connected learning experience. Consistently ranked among the nation’s top universities by U.S. News & World Report, Loyola is a STARS Gold-rated institution that is ranked as one of the country’s most sustainable campuses by The Princeton Review and has earned distinctions from AmeriCorps and the Carnegie Foundation for its longstanding record of service and community engagement. Guided by its Jesuit mission and commitment to caring for the whole person, Loyola educates ethical leaders who think critically, act with purpose, and strive to create a more just and sustainable world. Loyola University Chicago strives to be an employer of choice by offering its staff and faculty a wide array of affordable, comprehensive, and competitive benefits. To view our benefits in detail, click here . Loyola adheres to all applicable federal, state, and/or local civil rights laws and regulations prohibiting discrimination in private institutions of higher education. Please see the University’s Nondiscrimination Policy . About the Marcella Niehoff School of Nursing Loyola University Chicago’s Marcella Niehoff School of Nursing advances the science of nursing and provides a transformative education in the Jesuit Catholic tradition that prepares compassionate, innovative, diverse nurse leaders who provide care for the whole person and partner locally and globally to promote social justice and health for all. Our vision is to be a preeminent leader in transforming the health of persons, families, communities, and populations while promoting social justice, health equity, and quality care for all. A leader in nursing education for over 90 years, Loyola Nursing is a community of approximately 80 full-time faculty, 26 staff, and more than 1,300 students. Our programs include the BSN and Master’s Entry to Nursing (MN) programs for prelicensure students, a DNP with nurse practitioner tracks, and a PhD in Nursing. Loyola Nursing spans the University’s Lake Shore Campus and Health Sciences Campus. The Health Sciences Campus is also home to the Stritch School of Medicine, the Parkinson School of Health Sciences and Public Health, biomedical graduate programs, and Loyola’s health sciences research enterprise, including the 225,000-square-foot Center for Translational Research and Education. Loyola Nursing has earned national recognition for nursing education, research, and inclusive excellence. Recent distinctions include: A #19 national ranking for our BSN program in the 2027 U.S. News & World Report rankings, placing it among the top 3% of BSN programs nationwide. 2026 American Association of Colleges of Nursing ( AACN ) Exemplary Academic-Practice Partnership Award, recognizing Loyola Nursing’s partnership with Northwestern Medicine. 2024 AACN Inclusive Excellence, Belonging, and Sustainability in Nursing Education Award. Higher Education Excellence and Distinction ( HEED ) Awards for Health Professions Schools in 2023, 2024, 2025, and 2026. A new building scheduled to open on the Lake Shore Campus in 2028 will support the continued expansion of our BSN program. For more information, visit luc.edu/nursing . As a Jesuit Catholic institution of higher education, we seek candidates who will contribute to our strategic plan to deliver a Transformative Education in the Jesuit tradition. To learn more about Loyola University Chicago’s mission, candidates should consult our website at www.luc.edu/mission/. For information about the university’s focus on transformative education, they should consult our website at www.luc.edu/transformativeed. Quick Link for Posting https://www.careers.luc.edu/postings/34672 Job Number 8501017 Organizational Location HEALTH SCIENCES CAMPUS Posting Details Posting Detail Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date 01/01/2027 Position End Date Open Until Filled Yes Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Inclusive Excellence Statement Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Do you currently hold, or are you eligible for, RN licensure in the State of Illinois? Yes No Do you currently hold, or are you eligible for, APRN licensure in the State of Illinois? Yes No Are you certified as a Family Nurse Practitioner? Yes No Where did you hear about this position?\r\n•\tColleague\r\n•\tAmerican Association of Colleges of Nursing\r\n•\tNational Organization of Nurse Practitioner Faculties\r\n•\tMidwest Nursing Research Society\r\n•\tSouthern Nursing Research Society\r\n•\tNational Black Nurses Association\r\n•\tNational Association of Hispanic Nurses\r\n•\tPhilipine Nurses Association of America\r\n•\tAmerican Academy of Nursing\r\n•\tNational Association of Diversity Officers in \r\n        Higher Education\r\n•\tChronicle of Higher Education\r\n•\tOther, please specify: (Open Ended Question) Loyola University Chicago | Information: (773) 508 - 6000 1032 W Sheridan Rd | Chicago, IL | 60660 2018 (c) Loyola University Chicago | University Policies Support LUC To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
